              caller does not have the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__NNIICCEE privilege.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       Linux.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       Linux 2.6.7.

       Support  for  huge page policy was added with Linux 2.6.16.  For inter‐
       leave policy to be effective on huge page mappings the policied  memory
       needs to be tens of megabytes or larger.

       Before Linux 5.7.  MMPPOOLL__MMFF__SSTTRRIICCTT was ignored on huge page mappings.

       MMPPOOLL__MMFF__MMOOVVEE  and  MMPPOOLL__MMFF__MMOOVVEE__AALLLL  are available only on Linux 2.6.16
       and later.

NNOOTTEESS
       For information on library support, see nnuummaa(7).

       NUMA policy is not supported on a memory‐mapped  file  range  that  was
       mapped with the MMAAPP__SSHHAARREEDD flag.

       The  MMPPOOLL__DDEEFFAAUULLTT  mode  can  have  different  effects  for mmbbiinndd() and
       sseett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy(2).  When MMPPOOLL__DDEEFFAAUULLTT is specified for sseett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy(2),
       the thread’s memory policy reverts to the system default policy or  lo‐
       cal  allocation.   When MMPPOOLL__DDEEFFAAUULLTT is specified for a range of memory
       using mmbbiinndd(), any pages subsequently allocated for that range will use
       the thread’s memory policy, as set by  sseett__mmeemmppoolliiccyy(2).   This  effec‐
       tively  removes  the explicit policy from the specified range, "falling
       back" to a possibly nondefault policy.  To select explicit "local allo‐
       cation" for a memory range, specify a _m_o_d_e of MMPPOOLL__LLOOCCAALL  or  MMPPOOLL__PPRREE‐‐
       FFEERRRREEDD  with an empty set of nodes.  This method will work for sseett__mmeemm‐‐
       ppoolliiccyy(2), as well.

